;; Known, explained disagreements between PostgreSQL 17.7 and pg-datahike
;; found by datahike.fuzz.differential. One entry per sample:
;;   {:surface :select|:prepared|:join|:dml, :key <the SQL as generated>, :reason "..."}
;; or, for a gap the grammar reaches through many combinations that all mean
;; the same thing:
;;   {:surface :join, :key-pattern "<regex over the SQL>", :reason "..."}
;;
;; A disagreement NOT listed here fails the run (regression). A listed :key
;; entry that the run draws and that now AGREES also fails (baseline drift):
;; prune it and record the newly supported behaviour. A :key-pattern entry is
;; NOT drift-checked -- some samples of a class agree by accident (a WHERE
;; that empties the result) -- so prune it by hand when its gap closes. Keep
;; reasons specific -- "unsupported" is not a reason; say what is missing.
[{:surface :select
  :key "SELECT id, to_char(d, 'YYYY-MM-DD') AS c FROM ft ORDER BY id"
  :reason "to_char over date/timestamp is refused with 0A000: PostgreSQL's
           formatting.c template language (YYYY, MM, Day, FM, TH, ...) is not
           implemented. Numeric to_char works."}
 {:surface :join
  :key-pattern "^SELECT count\\(.*FROM ft FULL JOIN"
  :reason "an aggregate over a FULL JOIN is refused with 0A000, whatever the ON
           clause says: the join is two queries whose rows are concatenated, so
           an aggregate pushed into the halves is computed per half and answered
           twice (count(*) said 5 and 4 where PostgreSQL says 7). Refused until
           the combination happens before aggregation -- one query with two
           branches. A CLASS entry: the ON-clause grammar reaches this one
           refusal through dozens of combinations that all mean the same thing."}
 {:surface :join
  :key-pattern "FULL JOIN fu ON \\((?![^)]*(ft\\.\\w+ = fu\\.|fu\\.\\w+ = ft\\.))[^)]*\\) WHERE "
  :reason "a FULL JOIN whose ON clause has no equality BETWEEN the relations is
           0A000 here, as it is in PostgreSQL -- except when a WHERE clause
           rejects the null-extended rows of one side. PostgreSQL's
           reduce_outer_joins then weakens the join to LEFT/RIGHT/INNER, which
           needs no merge- or hash-joinable condition, and answers; we still
           refuse. Join-strength reduction from a strict WHERE qual is not
           implemented. The pattern matches only the ON clauses WITHOUT such an
           equality, so a wrong answer to a FULL JOIN that does have one is
           still a regression."}]
